# Work and trade by job (doc 30): each place's own work. Frosthollow's mines, Ridgemere's wharf, salt pans and Rain
# Hall, Coldwater's lake, Larkfield's market and carters, Mirebridge's fen, Southmeadow's fields, Oakhaven's quiet.
# Plain lines answer each other in parallel.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Frosthollow: mine and forge

scene tr_cb_ore_price
when topic=work|prices here.region=frosthollow
a : The Grayrock buyer knocked a penny off the ore again. | Ridgemere wants iron and won't pay for the mountain it comes out of. | They weighed my cart wet and paid me dry.
b[miner] : We should stop digging for a week. See how they like it.
b : Thieves in good cloth. | Cities never do. | Classic.
a : Thieves with a House seal. | They'll pay when the mountain stops giving. | Next time I'll weigh it in the snow.

scene tr_cb_nail_shrine
when topic=work here.region=frosthollow
a : Left a nail at the chapel door this morning. | The chapel door's thick with nails. Good season. | Someone took a nail from the chapel door. Took it!
b : Safe shift, then. | Good season. | Who'd dare?
a : Safe shift. Every shift. Bright One willing. | The Bright One's been kind. | Someone who'll be sorry below.

scene tr_cb_hot_anvil
when topic=work|smalltalk here.region=frosthollow here.place=tavern
a : Loudest argument in the Whitefangs was here last night. Over a pick. | The Frozen Lantern's out of ale. Frosthollow in mourning. | Miners at one end, smiths at the other. Same as ever.
b : Who won? | Mourning indeed. | Never mix.
a : The pick. It was broken over someone's head. | Till the carter comes up the High Road. | They mix at the end. Usually in a fight.

scene tr_cb_lamp
when topic=work here.region=frosthollow a.job=crafter|miner|merchant
a : Mended forty lamps this week. The pits eat them. | Brass is short. I'm patching lamps with tin. | A miner's lamp should burn a whole shift. These don't.
b : Good trade. | Will they hold? | Blame the oil.
a : Good trade in a bad business. | Better than the dark. | Blame the oil, the wick, and the mountain.

scene tr_cb_charcoal_burner
when topic=work here.region=frosthollow a.job=laborer|woodcutter
a : The charcoal smoke's in everything. My bread tastes of it. | Burned two clamps this week. The forge took both. | My eyes are red as a forge.
b : Everything tastes of it here. | They always want more. | Wash them.
a : Even the honey. | More and faster. | In what? The brook's frozen.

scene tr_ft_northern
when topic=work here.region=frosthollow a.role=guard
a : Snow on the wall again. In this season! | Watched the pass all night. Nothing moves up there. | Wind up here could peel a wolf.
b : North for you. | Good. | Peel a stone.
a : North's not for anyone. | Nothing's the hardest thing to watch. | Peeled me twice already.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Ridgemere: salt pans, wharf and Rain Hall

scene tr_sr_salt_pans
when topic=work here.region=ridgemere
a : Raked salt all morning. My pads are cracked to bleeding. | The pans need sun and we've had a week of rain. | Salt house is near full. Prices will drop.
b : Grease them. | Rain coast. | Good for buyers.
a : Grease and salt. Stings worse. | Rain coast, rain salt, no salt. | Good for buyers, bad for rakers.

scene tr_sr_vesk
when topic=work here.region=ridgemere a.job=laborer|fisher|merchant
a : The Customs House clerk came by. He counted every sack. | Owe the Pawn and Ledger more than a season's salt. | Greatly ships our salt and calls it theirs.
b : He always counts. | Everyone does. | Their stamp, our sweat.
a : And always comes up one short. | And next season too. | Their stamp, their coin, our sweat.

scene tr_sr_brine_barrel
when topic=work|smalltalk here.region=ridgemere here.place=tavern
a : The Tarred Rope's full of sailors' songs again. | Smell of tar and smoke and wet coats. Home. | Somebody's singing the drowned-boat song. Bad luck.
b : Nobody admits to knowing them. | Home sweet home. | Shut them up.
a : Everybody knows them by the third cup. | Home sour home. | You try telling a sailor to shut up.

scene tr_sr_gulls
when topic=work here.region=ridgemere a.job=fisher|sailor|laborer
a : Gulls flying inland. Storm by night. | The gulls stole half my catch off the slab. | Gulls quiet today. Fair weather.
b : They're never wrong. | Thieves with wings. | Go out, then.
a : Never. Gulls and old knees. | Fat, loud thieves. | Already gone. Just came back for my net.

scene tr_rm_quay
when topic=work here.region=ridgemere here.place=shore|work
a : Unloaded cedar from the Rainwood and iron for the yard. | The wharf's flooded at the spring tide. | Smokehouses running all night. Fish and smoke.
b : Busy wharf. | Wet feet. | Smell carries.
a : Busy and wet. | Wet everything. | Up to the Rain Hall.

scene tr_sf_wharf
when topic=work here.region=ridgemere here.place=shore|work|market
a : The wharf's thick with coasters. | Unloaded wine for the Heights. Not a drop for us. | Tide's low. Boats grounding.
b : Busy season. | Not a drop? | Bad for trade.
a : Busy and wet. | Not a drop. Smelled it all day. | Bad for boats. Good for haulers.

scene tr_rm_rain_hall
when topic=work here.region=ridgemere a.job=official|noble|servant
a : Rain's coming through the Rain Hall roof onto the council table. | The Houses met and agreed on nothing. | Buckets in the council chamber. Every session.
b : Fitting. | As usual. | They should mend it.
a : Fitting and wet. | Nothing and dinner. | They'd have to agree to.

scene tr_ua_pilgrim_trade
when topic=work here.region=ridgemere a.role=merchant
a : Sailors buy any charm with the Bright One's mark on it. | Sold candles to the Chapel of the Tide at a loss. Good for my soul. | City Office clerks check my permit every week.
b : Do they work? | And your purse? | Every week?
a : They comfort. That's the work. | My purse disagrees with my soul. | Every week, and it's always in order.

scene tr_sf_white_plaster
when topic=work here.region=ridgemere a.job=crafter|laborer
a : Whitewashed the chapel steps. Rain washed it off. | Plastered three houses in the Heights this week. Forelegs are white to the shoulder. | Slates for a merchant's roof. All the way up the hill.
b : Do it again. | Like a ghost. | Long way up.
a : Do it again tomorrow. | A rich ghost. Three houses' worth. | Long way down.

scene tr_ft_wall
when topic=work here.region=ridgemere a.role=guard
a : Mortar's crumbling on the north wall. | The Watch House is counting quarrels again. | The captain inspects tomorrow. Polish everything.
b : Report it. | We're short? | Everything?
a : I did. Last winter. | Short of everything except orders. | Even the porridge.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Coldwater: fishers and boatwrights on Glasswater

scene tr_lk_boatwright
when topic=work here.region=coldwater
a : Finished a new hull. Steamed every plank myself. | Caulking a boat that leaks like a basket. | The lake's too low for launching.
b : Lovely lines. | Whose? | Wait for the melt.
a : Lovely lines and a leak I can't find. | The same fisher. Every season. | The melt comes. Late, but it comes.

scene tr_lk_ice_fishing
when topic=work here.region=coldwater here.season=winter
a : Cut a hole in the ice and sat on it all morning. | Ice cracked under my hind paws. Ran. | Pike through the ice. Fat as a pup.
b : Catch anything? | Gods! | Show me.
a : A chill and a perch. | Faster than I've run in years. | It's at home. Being cooked.

scene tr_lk_reeds
when topic=work here.region=lake a.job=fisher|crafter|laborer
a : Cutting reeds for thatch. Wet to the shoulders. | The reed beds are thick this year. | Thatchers want three loads by Restday.
b : Good roofs, though. | Good. | Three!
a : Good roofs, cold fishers. | Thick enough to hide a boat. | Three, and dry.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Larkfield: the market town of the downs

scene tr_wm_frontier
when topic=work here.region=larkfield
a : Every drover on the downs stops here. | A drover brought three hundred sheep through the square. | The general store's ledger has half the downs in it.
b : Good for trade. | Through the square? | Owing?
a : Good for trade, bad for the cobbles. | And left half of them on the cobbles. | Owing, and proud of it.

scene tr_wm_watch_house
when topic=work here.region=larkfield a.role=guard
a : The watch map shows a camp on the far downs. | Patrol came back with nothing but tired legs. | We're four for a whole town.
b : Bandits? | Better than the other thing. | Ask for more.
a : Or drovers. Hard to tell at that distance. | Tired legs and a sore head. | From whom? The city's a week off.

scene tr_wm_hide_yard
when topic=work here.region=larkfield a.job=crafter|laborer|merchant
a : The tanner's yard reeks. Wind's the wrong way. | Bought twenty hides off the drovers cheap. | Tanning's slow money but sure money.
b : Every day it reeks. | Good hides? | Sure as death.
a : Today it reeks loud. | Good enough for boots. | Sure, and it smells like it.

scene tr_wm_proud
when topic=work|smalltalk here.region=larkfield
a : We don't need a city. Never have. | City folk come through and ask where the shops are. | Nobody tells Larkfield what to do.
b : Proud of it. | They are the shops. | Except the weather.
a : Proud and poor. | And the cobbles. | The weather's from Larkfield too.

scene tr_ac_caravans
when topic=work here.region=larkfield
a : Counted eleven wagons in, nine out. | The Singing Lark's full of carters again. | The caravan shed's stacked to the roof.
b : Two stuck somewhere. | Good for the inn. | Busy season.
a : Two on the road, or in a ditch. | Good for the inn, loud for the rest. | Busy as a beehive. Twice as stung.

scene tr_ac_toll
when topic=work here.region=larkfield
a : The gate toll went up again. | Paid the toll, then the bridge, then the ferryman. | The toll keeper knows every carter by name and debt.
b : Robbery. | Three tolls! | He would.
a : Robbery with a seal on it. | Three tolls, one road. | And by the creak of their wheels.

scene tr_ac_stable
when topic=work here.region=larkfield a.job=herder|laborer|carter
a : Groomed twelve carters' horses before breakfast. | One of the carters' beasts kicked down a stall. | Hay's dear and the horses don't care.
b : Twelve! | Bad-tempered beast. | They never do.
a : Twelve and a mule. | Bad-tempered carter, more like. | Horses have it easy.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Mirebridge: peat and herbs in the Sallow Fens

scene tr_fh_peat
when topic=work here.region=mirebridge
a : Cut peat since dawn. My fur's black and my nose is full. | The cuttings filled with water overnight. | Peat stacks are drying nicely.
b : Warm winter, though. | Bail it. | Good.
a : Warm winter, black summer. | Bail a bog? | Good, till the rain.

scene tr_fh_herbalist
when topic=work here.region=mirebridge a.job=healer|merchant|farmer
a : City herbalists buy our bog moss and sell it as a cure. | Picked sundew till my eyes went cross. | Marsh mallow's in flower. Best week of the year.
b : At ten times the price. | What's it for? | Pick it all.
a : Twenty times. | Coughs. And warts. And it eats flies. | Leave some. Next year.

scene tr_fh_fog
when topic=work|weather here.region=mirebridge
a : Fog so thick I walked into the dyke. | Can't see the next tussock. Stayed home. | Fen lights out in the fog again last night.
b : You smell of it. | Good choice. | Don't follow them.
a : Bog and shame. | Lost a day's peat, though. | Nobody who follows comes back.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Southmeadow: grain, mill and the muddy road

scene tr_af_ford
when topic=work here.region=southmeadow
a : The road's mud to the axles. Carters stuck both sides of town. | Took a penny toll from a lord's carriage at the gate. He wasn't pleased. | Road's dried out. Easy going.
b : Wait it out. | Did he pay? | Good.
a : They'll wait. And grumble. | Paid in copper and insults. | Easy for them. Busy for us.

scene tr_af_harvest
when topic=work here.region=southmeadow here.season=summer|autumn
a : Southmeadow wheat, gold to the horizon. | Every pup in town's out binding sheaves. | The granary's full. Fuller than I've seen.
b : Prettiest sight. | Even the little ones? | Good year.
a : And the hardest work. | Even the little ones. They're slow. | Good year. Watch the prices drop.

scene tr_af_mill
when topic=work here.region=farmland a.job=farmer|laborer|merchant
a : The mill wheel's jammed with weed. | Miller wants a sack in ten for his toll. | Ground grain all night. The stones were singing.
b : Clear it. | Ten! | Good flour?
a : In that cold water? | Ten, and he sweeps the floor for himself. | Fine as smoke.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Oakhaven: the quiet forest town

scene tr_hm_everyone
when topic=work|smalltalk here.region=oakhaven
a : Everyone here has two jobs. Nobody's idle. | Needed a wheelwright. There isn't one. Went to Mirebridge. | Oakhaven has one of everything and none spare.
b : Two's plenty. | Long walk. | Like life.
a : Till someone gets ill. | Long walk for a spoke. | Like everything.

scene tr_hm_quiet
when topic=work here.region=oakhaven
a : Nothing happens here. I like it. | A stranger came through. Biggest news in a month. | Fed the pigs, mended the wall, ate supper. Busy day.
b : Me too. | Who? | That's busy?
a : Don't tell anyone. They'll come. | Didn't stop. Didn't say. | Busy for Oakhaven.
